/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Myths & Religions (batch z) */
WB.L("jainism", "beliefs", "beginner", "Jainism: the path of harming nothing",
  "Some monks sweep the ground before every step so they won't crush an insect. Behind that gesture lies one of the world's oldest and most demanding ideas: that every living thing has a soul.",
  [
    ["An ancient Indian faith", "Jainism is an Indian religion built on non-violence, self-discipline and freeing the soul.",
      "Jainism grew up on the Ganges plain of northern India, in the same restless era as the Buddha. Its most famous teacher, Mahavira, is usually dated to the sixth or fifth century BCE. Jains do not call him the founder, though. In their tradition he is the twenty-fourth and last Tirthankara of our age, a \"ford-maker\" who crossed the river of rebirth and showed others the way. His predecessor, Parshvanatha, is widely accepted by historians as a real figure who lived a few centuries earlier.\n\nToday there are roughly four to five million Jains, mostly in India, with communities in Britain, North America, East Africa and Australia."],
    ["Souls everywhere", "Jains believe souls exist in all living things and are weighed down by karma, a fine matter that clings to them.",
      "At the heart of Jain teaching is the jiva, the soul. Every soul is by nature perfectly knowing, perceiving and blissful. The problem is karma. In many religions karma is a moral law of cause and effect. In Jainism it is an extremely fine substance that clings to the soul whenever we act with passion, greed or violence.\n\nThat build-up keeps the soul trapped in the cycle of birth and death. Souls are found not only in humans and animals but in plants, and even in tiny beings in earth, water, fire and air. The goal of life is to stop new karma attaching and to burn away the old, until the soul rises free."],
    ["Ahimsa: the first vow", "Ahimsa, or non-violence, is the central Jain value and covers diet, daily habits, words and thoughts.",
      "Because so many beings have souls, the Jain duty not to harm reaches further than in almost any other tradition. Jains are strict vegetarians. Many also avoid root vegetables such as onions, garlic and potatoes, because pulling them up kills the whole plant and the small creatures in the soil. Traditionally many avoid eating after sunset, when insects could fall unseen into food.\n\nMonks and nuns may carry a soft brush to clear the ground before they sit or walk, and some wear a cloth over the mouth to avoid harming tiny beings in the air. Harsh speech and hateful intentions count as harm too, so ahimsa is a discipline of the mind as much as the body."],
    ["Many-sided truth", "Anekantavada teaches that truth has many sides and no single viewpoint captures all of it.",
      "One of Jainism's most admired ideas is anekantavada, often translated as \"many-sidedness\". Reality is complex, Jain philosophers argued, and any statement about it is true only from a particular standpoint. To illustrate this, Jains, like Buddhists and Hindus, tell the story of the blind men and the elephant. One touches the trunk and says an elephant is like a snake. Another feels a leg and says it is like a tree. Each is partly right and wrong to think he has the whole truth.\n\nJain logicians built this into a careful system for qualifying statements with words like \"in some respect\". Many modern Jains see it as a partner to ahimsa: intellectual humility as a form of non-violence."],
    ["Branches, temples and reach", "Two branches differ over monastic dress, yet Jain ideas of non-violence shaped Gandhi and still inspire charity.",
      "Monks and nuns take five great vows: non-violence, truthfulness, not stealing, chastity and non-possession. How far to take non-possession helped split Jainism in two. Digambara (\"sky-clad\") monks go naked and hold that women must be reborn as men to reach liberation. Svetambara (\"white-clad\") monks and nuns wear simple white robes, and the tradition teaches that women can be liberated. Lay Jains follow gentler versions of the vows.\n\nJain temples are known for carved marble, as at Dilwara and Ranakpur. The festival of Paryushana ends with the words \"Micchami Dukkadam\", roughly \"may my wrongs be forgiven\". Gandhi, raised in Gujarat, counted the Jain thinker Shrimad Rajchandra among his guides. Jain communities also fund hospitals for animals and birds, including one in Old Delhi."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do Jains regard Mahavira?", ["As the founder who started Jainism from scratch","As a god who grants wishes to worshippers","As the last Tirthankara, or ford-maker","As a famous disciple of the Buddha"], 2, "In Jain tradition Mahavira is the twenty-fourth and last Tirthankara of our age, not the founder."],
    [1, "How does Jainism picture karma?", ["As a fine substance that clings to the soul","As a purely moral law with no physical effect","As a reward given by a creator god","As something that affects only humans"], 0, "Karma is pictured as extremely fine matter that attaches to the soul through passion, greed and violence."],
    [2, "Why do many Jains avoid root vegetables?", ["They are thought to be unclean","Uprooting them kills the plant and soil life","They are said to grow only in the dark","Monks are forbidden all vegetables"], 1, "Harm to any living being counts, so uprooting a plant and the creatures around it is avoided."],
    [3, "What does the blind men and elephant story illustrate?", ["Each view holds part of the truth","Only the man at the trunk was right","Animals see reality better than us","No truth can ever be known at all"], 0, "Anekantavada holds that each standpoint is partly right, so no one should claim the whole truth."],
    [4, "What helped divide Jainism into two main branches?", ["Whether Jains should eat meat","How far to take the vow of non-possession","Disagreement over Mahavira's birthplace","A war between two royal families"], 1, "Digambara monks give up even clothing, while Svetambara monks and nuns wear simple white robes."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Some monks sweep the path before each step. The reason is older than it sounds."});

WB.L("irish-myths", "beliefs", "beginner", "Ireland's gods, heroes and fairy mounds",
  "Before St Patrick, Ireland had shining gods who vanished into the hills, a hero who could fight an army alone, and a giant who supposedly built a causeway to Scotland.",
  [
    ["Stories saved by monks", "Most Irish myths survive only because medieval Christian scribes wrote them down, and they reshaped them as they did.",
      "The pre-Christian Irish left no written scriptures. Poets and druids, trained for years, carried genealogies, laws and tales in memory. What we have comes mostly from manuscripts copied by monks between roughly the eighth and fifteenth centuries, long after Ireland became Christian.\n\nThat makes the stories wonderful but tricky. The scribes preserved them with affection, yet they also reshaped them, turning old gods into ancient kings and fitting Irish history into a biblical timeline. Scholars sort the tales into groups: the Mythological Cycle, the Ulster Cycle, the Fenian Cycle and the Cycles of the Kings."],
    ["Invaders and shining ones", "The Tuatha Dé Danann were Ireland's old gods, remembered in the Book of Invasions as a magical people who retreated into the hills.",
      "The medieval Lebor Gabála Érenn, the \"Book of the Taking of Ireland\", tells of Ireland settled in waves: Cessair and Partholón, then Nemed, then the Fir Bolg, each struggling against the monstrous Fomorians. Then came the Tuatha Dé Danann, magical beings who arrived wrapped in mist, and finally the Milesians, ancestors of the Irish. Compiled largely in the eleventh century, it is legend, not a record of real migrations.\n\nMany scholars see the Tuatha Dé as former gods. The Dagda owned a cauldron that never ran empty. Lugh, master of every craft, led them to victory over the Fomorians. Brigid, linked with poetry and healing, lives on as St Brigid. The Morrígan, goddess of war, could become a crow. When the Milesians won, the Tuatha Dé withdrew into the sídhe, the ancient mounds, and became the fairy folk of folklore."],
    ["Cú Chulainn, the Hound", "The boy hero Cú Chulainn earned his name by killing a guard dog, then held back an army single-handed.",
      "The boy Sétanta, nephew of King Conchobar of Ulster, arrived late to a feast at the smith Culann's house. Culann's ferocious guard dog attacked him, and he killed it. Seeing the smith's grief, the boy offered to be his hound until a new one was raised. So he became Cú Chulainn, \"Culann's Hound\".\n\nIn battle he could be seized by a terrifying frenzy, the ríastrad, which twisted his body into a monstrous shape. In the epic Táin Bó Cúailnge, the \"Cattle Raid of Cooley\", Queen Medb of Connacht invades Ulster to seize a prize bull. The Ulster warriors lie helpless under a curse, so the teenage Cú Chulainn holds back her army alone, fighting a long series of duels at a river ford."],
    ["A hero's death", "Bound by conflicting geasa, Cú Chulainn tied himself to a standing stone to die on his feet.",
      "Irish heroes lived under geasa, sacred prohibitions that could not be broken without disaster. Cú Chulainn was bound never to eat dog meat, yet also never to refuse hospitality. Before his last battle, three old women offered him dog meat, and either choice meant breaking a geis. Weakened, he fought on.\n\nMortally wounded, he tied himself to a standing stone so that he could die on his feet, facing his enemies. They dared not approach until a crow, the sign of the Morrígan, landed on his shoulder. A bronze statue of the dying Cú Chulainn stands in Dublin's General Post Office as a memorial to those who fought in the 1916 Easter Rising."],
    ["Fionn, swans and Youth", "Fionn gained wisdom from the Salmon of Knowledge, while tales of Lir's children and Oisín explore time and loss.",
      "The Fenian Cycle follows Fionn mac Cumhaill and his war band. As a youth he served a poet who had spent seven years trying to catch the Salmon of Knowledge. Told to cook it but not taste it, Fionn burnt his thumb, sucked it, and gained all its wisdom. Later folklore made him a giant who built the Giant's Causeway as a road to Scotland, a playful tale about land formed by cooling lava.\n\nIn the Children of Lir, a jealous stepmother turns four children into swans for nine hundred years. When the spell breaks, they age and die. Fionn's son Oisín rides to Tír na nÓg, the Land of Youth. After what seems three years, he visits home and finds three hundred have passed. Touching the ground, he ages instantly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why are Irish myths tricky to treat as pure pre-Christian belief?", ["Monks wrote them down and reshaped them","They were composed by Victorian folklorists","They survive only as spoken tales today","They were carved in stone by the druids"], 0, "Monks preserved the tales but turned gods into kings and fitted them into a biblical timeline."],
    [1, "What happened to the Tuatha Dé Danann after the Milesians won?", ["They sailed back across the sea to Spain","They were wiped out in a final battle","They withdrew into the mounds as fairy folk","They became the first Irish Christian saints"], 2, "The old gods retreated into the sídhe, which is how they became the fairy folk of later folklore."],
    [2, "How did Sétanta become Cú Chulainn?", ["He won the Cattle Raid of Cooley","He killed a guard dog, then took its place","He was cursed by Queen Medb","He was adopted by the smith's family"], 1, "After killing Culann's dog, he offered to serve as the smith's hound, earning the name."],
    [3, "Why was Cú Chulainn doomed before his last battle?", ["He lost his weapons while crossing a river","Each choice would break one of his sacred vows","His own army deserted him on the eve of battle","Heavy snow blocked every road to the battlefield"], 1, "Eating dog meat or refusing hospitality each broke a geis, so either choice meant disaster."],
    [4, "What happens to Oisín when he touches the ground in Ireland?", ["He returns safely to Tír na nÓg","He is turned into a swan","He ages at once into an old man","He is turned into a standing stone"], 2, "Three hundred years had passed in Ireland, and touching the ground let that time catch up with him."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A hero who held off an army tied himself to a stone. Why wouldn't they approach?"});

WB.L("king-arthur-legend", "beliefs", "beginner", "King Arthur: how a legend was built",
  "There may never have been a King Arthur. Yet over a thousand years, writers in Wales, England and France assembled Camelot, the Round Table and the Holy Grail piece by piece.",
  [
    ["Faint traces after Rome", "Arthur's story grew from very thin records in the obscure centuries after Roman rule ended in Britain.",
      "Roman authority in Britain collapsed in the early fifth century. Over the next two hundred years, Anglo-Saxon groups settled much of what became England, while Britons held out in the west and north. Written records are extremely thin, which is why legends could flourish.\n\nA British monk, Gildas, writing in the sixth century, described a British victory over the Saxons at Mount Badon. Tellingly, he never mentions Arthur. The earliest possible reference is in a Welsh poem, Y Gododdin, which praises a warrior as brave, \"though he was no Arthur\". Its date is uncertain and the line may be a later addition. From these faint traces, a towering figure would grow."],
    ["Twelve battles", "Early Welsh texts present Arthur as a war leader, though historians doubt there is enough evidence to prove he existed.",
      "Around 830, the Latin Historia Brittonum, traditionally linked to a monk named Nennius, listed twelve battles fought by Arthur, a \"leader of battles\", ending at Badon. Some sites are impossible to locate, and one passage has him carrying an image of the Virgin Mary into combat.\n\nA later Welsh chronicle, the Annales Cambriae, records Badon and also Camlann, \"in which Arthur and Medraut fell\". Medraut would become the treacherous Mordred. Early Welsh poems and tales show a different Arthur, leading heroes against giants, witches and a monstrous boar. Most historians now think there is not enough evidence to say whether a real Arthur existed, though the debate continues."],
    ["Geoffrey's bestseller", "Geoffrey of Monmouth's History turned Arthur into a conquering king and gave the story its famous outline.",
      "The Arthur most people know was launched by Geoffrey of Monmouth, a cleric in Oxford. His History of the Kings of Britain, completed around 1136, claimed to be translated from an ancient British book, a claim most scholars doubt.\n\nGeoffrey gave Arthur a dramatic life. Merlin's magic lets Uther Pendragon visit Igerna at Tintagel, and Arthur is conceived. As king, Arthur defeats the Saxons, conquers much of Europe and challenges Rome itself. While he is away, his nephew Mordred seizes the throne and Guinevere. Arthur returns, kills Mordred and is mortally wounded, then carried to the island of Avalon to be healed. The book was a medieval sensation, surviving in more than two hundred manuscripts."],
    ["Knights, table and Grail", "French writers added the Round Table, Lancelot, Camelot and the Holy Grail, shifting focus to the knights.",
      "Writers across the Channel dressed Geoffrey's warrior king in courtly fashion. In 1155 the Norman poet Wace retold the history in French verse and introduced the Round Table, where no knight could claim the place of honour. Later, Chrétien de Troyes shifted attention from Arthur to his knights. He gave us the earliest surviving story of Lancelot's love for Guinevere, and first mentioned Camelot.\n\nChrétien's unfinished Perceval, from the 1180s, introduced a mysterious graal, a serving dish carried in a procession. He died before explaining it. Soon Robert de Boron identified the Grail as the vessel of the Last Supper, and later romances made the quest a spiritual test that most knights failed. The pure Galahad, Lancelot's son, achieves it."],
    ["Malory and the returning king", "Malory fixed the English legend in 1485, and Arthur keeps returning because each age reshapes him.",
      "In 1191, monks at Glastonbury Abbey announced they had found the grave of Arthur and Guinevere. The abbey needed funds and pilgrims after a fire, and many historians suspect the find was staged. It still tied Avalon to Glastonbury. Nearly three centuries later, Sir Thomas Malory, who wrote at least part of his work in prison, gathered the French and English stories into one English prose narrative. William Caxton printed it in 1485 as Le Morte d'Arthur, and it became the main source for later English retellings.\n\nLegend said Arthur would return in Britain's hour of need, and he has. Tennyson made him a model of Victorian duty. T. H. White used him to think about power after two world wars. Arthur stands for the hope of a just ruler, and the sadness when good kingdoms fall."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why could Arthur legends flourish in the centuries after Rome?", ["Written records from the period are extremely thin","Roman historians wrote detailed biographies of him","Anglo-Saxons recorded every battle","Gildas named Arthur at Badon"], 0, "With so few records, there was little to contradict the growing stories."],
    [1, "What do most historians conclude about a real Arthur?", ["He was certainly a Roman general in Britain","There is not enough evidence to say either way","Gildas confirms he led the British at Badon","Geoffrey of Monmouth certainly invented him"], 1, "The early sources are thin and contradictory, so the question remains open."],
    [2, "What did Geoffrey of Monmouth's book do for the legend?", ["Showed Arthur was only a Welsh folk hero","Proved the stories were invented by French poets","Gave Arthur a dramatic life as a conquering king","Replaced Arthur with Lancelot"], 2, "Geoffrey's history launched the Arthur most people know, from Merlin and Uther to Mordred and Avalon."],
    [3, "How did the Grail change in the romances?", ["From a mysterious dish to the vessel of the Last Supper","From the vessel of the Last Supper to a mysterious dish","From a royal crown to the vessel of the Last Supper","From a mysterious dish to a mythical sword"], 0, "Chrétien introduced a mysterious dish, and Robert de Boron made it Christ's Last Supper vessel."],
    [4, "Why does the Arthur legend keep returning?", ["Historians keep finding new graves","Each age reshapes him for itself","Malory's book has never gone out of print","Living kings still claim descent from him"], 1, "Authors from Tennyson to White reused Arthur to explore duty, power and hope."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The Round Table and Camelot were added by French poets. Was there ever a king?"});

WB.L("great-pilgrimages", "beliefs", "beginner", "The great pilgrimages",
  "Every year millions of people walk, pray and queue for days to reach a single sacred place. Pilgrimage is one of the oldest things humans do, and it is booming.",
  [
    ["Why people make the journey", "Pilgrimage joins an outer journey to an inner one, and the hardship is often part of the point.",
      "A pilgrimage is a journey to a place believed to be holy. People make it to fulfil a duty, give thanks, ask for healing or forgiveness, honour the dead, or simply be near something sacred.\n\nAlmost every major tradition has sites: Jerusalem for Jews, Christians and Muslims; Mecca for Muslims; the Ganges for Hindus; Bodh Gaya, where the Buddha is said to have awakened, for Buddhists; the Golden Temple at Amritsar for Sikhs. The hardship is often part of the point. Walking long distances, fasting or sleeping rough strips away daily comforts, and many pilgrims say the road changes them as much as the destination."],
    ["The Hajj", "The Hajj is a once-in-a-lifetime duty for able Muslims, where rich and poor stand dressed alike.",
      "The Hajj, one of the Five Pillars of Islam, is a duty for every Muslim who is physically and financially able. It takes place over several days in the month of Dhu al-Hijjah, and in recent years has drawn between one and a half and two and a half million pilgrims.\n\nBefore arriving, pilgrims enter a state of ritual purity called ihram. Men wear two plain, unstitched white cloths, so rich and poor stand alike before God. Pilgrims circle the Kaaba seven times anticlockwise. The most important moment is a day standing in prayer on the plain of Arafat. They also throw pebbles at pillars in Mina, remembering Abraham's rejection of temptation. Eid al-Adha follows, celebrated by Muslims worldwide."],
    ["Rivers and the Kumbh Mela", "Hindu pilgrims bathe in the Ganges at the Kumbh Mela, and many hope to die or be cremated in Varanasi.",
      "Hindu tradition says gods and demons churned the cosmic ocean for amrita, the nectar of immortality. Drops spilled from the pot, or kumbha, at four places: Prayagraj, Haridwar, Ujjain and Nashik. The Kumbh Mela rotates among them, and the largest gatherings, at Prayagraj, draw tens of millions over several weeks. Pilgrims believe bathing at auspicious times washes away sins. Organisers build a temporary city with roads, bridges, hospitals and police stations. UNESCO recognised the festival in 2017.\n\nVaranasi, on the Ganges, is among the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities and is Shiva's city. Many Hindus believe dying there, or having ashes placed in the river, brings moksha, release from rebirth. Cremation fires at Manikarnika ghat burn day and night."],
    ["Camino and Shikoku", "The Camino de Santiago and Japan's Shikoku circuit show how walking journeys of faith have endured for centuries.",
      "According to tradition, the remains of St James were discovered in north-western Spain in the ninth century, and a cathedral rose at Santiago de Compostela. Medieval pilgrims walked from all over Europe along the Camino. The scallop shell became their symbol, and yellow arrows still guide walkers. Hundreds of thousands now arrive each year; those who walk the final 100 kilometres, or cycle 200, can receive a certificate called the Compostela.\n\nOn Japan's island of Shikoku, a loop of about 1,200 kilometres links 88 Buddhist temples, honouring the monk Kūkai. Pilgrims, called henro, wear white and carry a staff said to represent Kūkai walking beside them. Walking it takes one to two months. Locals offer pilgrims gifts of food or money, a custom called osettai."],
    ["Pilgrimage today", "Cheaper travel has made pilgrimage bigger, bringing safety and crowding problems, but the need for special places remains.",
      "Cheaper travel has transformed pilgrimage. Journeys that once took months now take hours, and numbers have grown enormously. That brings pressure. Saudi Arabia allocates Hajj places by country through a quota and has built vast infrastructure to move crowds safely, yet crushes and extreme heat remain serious dangers at crowded sites. Popular Camino routes face overcrowding, and towns worry about housing and daily life.\n\nNew kinds of pilgrimage have appeared too. Many Australians and New Zealanders travel to Gallipoli for Anzac Day dawn services, and visitors file past the graves of musicians and writers. Whether religious or not, people still seem to need special places where they feel connected to something larger than themselves."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What role does hardship play in pilgrimage?", ["It shows that the journey has failed","It is avoided by most modern pilgrims","It is often part of the journey's purpose","It is added only by modern organisers"], 2, "Walking, fasting and sleeping rough are often part of the point, and the road itself changes pilgrims."],
    [1, "Why do male Hajj pilgrims wear plain white unstitched cloths?", ["Rich and poor stand dressed alike before God","It marks who has completed the Hajj before","They must be easy to spot in crowds","It shows which country they come from"], 0, "The simple dress of ihram removes signs of wealth and status."],
    [2, "What do many Hindus believe about Varanasi?", ["It may be visited only once in a lifetime","Dying there is believed to bring moksha","Only ascetics may bathe in its waters","It was founded after the Kumbh Mela"], 1, "Release from the cycle of rebirth is believed to follow death in the holy city, or having ashes placed in the Ganges."],
    [3, "What does the staff carried by a Shikoku henro represent?", ["The monk Kūkai walking beside them","A permit from the temples","The distance of 88 temples","The scallop shell of Santiago"], 0, "The staff stands for Kūkai himself, which is why hats say 'we two, travelling together'."],
    [4, "What problem has cheaper travel brought to pilgrimage?", ["Far fewer people now choose to take part","Crowding and safety risks at popular sites","Sites have lost their religious meaning","Journeys now take far longer than before"], 1, "Numbers have grown enormously, bringing dangers such as crushes and heat, and strain on local towns."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why male Hajj pilgrims, rich or poor, all wear the same plain white cloth."});

WB.L("dia-de-muertos", "beliefs", "beginner", "Día de Muertos: a festival for the dead",
  "Each year at the start of November, families across Mexico build glowing altars and lay paths of orange petals so that their dead can find the way home. It is a celebration, not a horror show.",
  [
    ["Not Mexican Halloween", "Día de Muertos is a family celebration of love and remembrance, not a spooky festival.",
      "Día de Muertos, the Day of the Dead, is often lumped in with Halloween because the dates are close and both involve skulls. The spirit is very different. Halloween plays with fear; Día de Muertos is about love and remembrance.\n\nFamilies believe their departed relatives return for a short visit, and the living prepare to welcome them as honoured guests. The main days are 1 and 2 November. In many places the first day honours children who have died, sometimes called los angelitos, the little angels, and the second honours adults. Customs vary greatly from region to region, and Indigenous communities often keep some of the most distinctive traditions."],
    ["Two worlds meet", "The festival blends Indigenous Mexican ways of honouring the dead with Catholic feast days.",
      "Long before the Spanish arrived, peoples of Mesoamerica, including the Aztecs, held ceremonies honouring the dead. Death was seen as part of a cycle rather than a final end, and the Aztec underworld, Mictlan, was ruled by Mictlantecuhtli and his consort Mictecacihuatl.\n\nAfter the Spanish conquest in the sixteenth century, missionaries promoted the Catholic feasts of All Saints' Day and All Souls' Day on 1 and 2 November. Over centuries, local practices and Catholic customs wove together. Historians debate how much of today's festival comes directly from pre-Hispanic religion, since some features developed much later. What is clear is that the result is uniquely Mexican, neither simply Indigenous nor simply European."],
    ["The ofrenda", "An ofrenda is a home altar of photographs, favourite foods and objects that welcomes returning souls.",
      "The heart of the celebration is the ofrenda, an altar built in homes, and sometimes in schools, offices and public squares. It usually has several tiers covered in cloth and decorated with papel picado, delicate tissue paper cut into patterns.\n\nPhotographs of the dead take pride of place, along with things they loved in life: a favourite dish, a bottle of tequila, cigarettes, sweets, toys for children. Candles light the way, and copal incense perfumes the air. Many families include water to quench the souls' thirst after their long journey, and salt for purification. Building the ofrenda is a family task, and telling stories about the people pictured is part of the point. Remembering keeps the dead present."],
    ["Marigolds and sugar skulls", "Orange marigolds, sweet bread and cheerful sugar skulls express the idea that death comes to everyone and can be met with a smile.",
      "The flower of the season is the cempasúchil, a vivid orange marigold whose name comes from the Nahuatl language. Its strong colour and scent are believed to guide souls, and families scatter petals in paths from the street, or the cemetery, to the altar.\n\nBakeries fill with pan de muerto, a soft, sweet bread often flavoured with orange blossom and decorated with strips of dough shaped like bones. Sugar skulls, calaveras de azúcar, are moulded from sugar and decorated with coloured icing, sometimes with a person's name across the forehead. They are cheerful and bright rather than morbid. They express a familiar attitude: death comes to everyone, so it may as well be met with a smile."],
    ["Cemetery, Catrina and heritage", "The festival mixes warm graveside vigils with joking skeletons, and UNESCO recognised it in 2008.",
      "In many towns, families clean and decorate graves with marigolds, candles and offerings, and some keep vigil through the night. On Janitzio island in Lake Pátzcuaro, Purépecha fishermen cross the lake in canoes carrying lights. The mood is warm and social, closer to a family reunion than a funeral.\n\nSkeletons dressed as living people have long mocked the powerful in Mexico. In the early 1910s, printmaker José Guadalupe Posada etched a skeleton woman in a grand feathered hat, mocking Mexicans who imitated European fashion. Diego Rivera placed her in his 1947 mural, and she became La Catrina. UNESCO listed the festival in 2008. The film Spectre (2015) invented a Mexico City parade, which the city then staged for real, and Pixar's Coco (2017) spread the ofrenda worldwide."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does Día de Muertos differ from Halloween?", ["It welcomes the dead home with love","It is meant to frighten children","It is held on 31 October each year","It is a festival only for children"], 0, "The festival is a family celebration of remembrance, not a spooky one."],
    [1, "How did today's festival take shape?", ["It was created in the twentieth century for tourists","It came entirely from Spanish Catholic practice","It is a direct copy of Aztec ceremonies","Indigenous and Catholic customs blended"], 3, "Local traditions and Catholic feast days blended into something uniquely Mexican."],
    [2, "What is the purpose of the water and food on an ofrenda?", ["To raise money for the local church","To refresh the returning souls","To be left out for wandering animals","To decorate the altar and nothing more"], 1, "Favourite foods and water for thirst honour the dead, and telling stories keeps them present."],
    [3, "What attitude do cheerful sugar skulls express?", ["Dread of the dead returning each year","Death is a subject best kept hidden","Death comes to all, so meet it with a smile","Only the wealthy should be remembered"], 2, "The bright skulls reflect an acceptance of death rather than morbidity."],
    [4, "What was the original point of the skeleton that became La Catrina?", ["To warn children to stay away from graves","To mock those who copied European fashion","To celebrate the wealthiest families of the city","To honour the victims of the Revolution"], 1, "Posada's skeleton in a feathered hat satirised Mexicans copying European style and forgetting their roots."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why Mexican families lay a path of orange petals for the dead to follow."});

WB.L("bahai-faith", "beliefs", "intermediate", "The Bahá'í Faith: one God, one humanity",
  "Born in nineteenth-century Persia amid persecution and exile, the Bahá'í Faith teaches that all the great religions come from the same source. One of its temples stands among the gum trees of Sydney's northern beaches.",
  [
    ["The Báb", "The Bahá'í story begins in 1844, when a young Persian merchant declared a new age and was later executed.",
      "In 1844, in the Persian city of Shiraz, a young merchant named Siyyid ‘Alí-Muhammad declared himself the Báb, meaning \"the Gate\". He taught that a new era of revelation was beginning and that he was preparing the way for a far greater messenger. His message spread quickly, and so did opposition from religious and political authorities. Thousands of his followers were killed.\n\nThe Báb was imprisoned and, in 1850, executed by firing squad in Tabriz. Bahá'í accounts say the first volley only cut the ropes binding him, and a second regiment carried out the execution. Bahá'ís regard him as a messenger of God in his own right and the forerunner of their faith's founder."],
    ["Bahá'u'lláh in exile", "The founder, Bahá'u'lláh, spent about four decades as a prisoner and exile, writing the faith's central texts.",
      "Mírzá Husayn-‘Alí, born in 1817 to a noble Persian family, followed the Báb and took the title Bahá'u'lláh, \"the Glory of God\". In 1852 he was thrown into a notorious underground prison in Tehran, the Black Pit, where Bahá'ís believe he first received the revelation of his mission.\n\nHe was banished to Baghdad, then Constantinople, Adrianople and finally the prison city of Akka, in what is now Israel. In 1863, in a Baghdad garden called Ridván, he announced he was the messenger the Báb had foretold. He wrote thousands of tablets and several books. He died in 1892 near Akka, where his shrine is the holiest place on Earth for Bahá'ís."],
    ["Progressive revelation", "Bahá'ís believe one God has revealed truth in stages through messengers suited to their times.",
      "A central teaching is that there is one God, who has revealed truth to humanity in stages. Messengers such as Abraham, Moses, Krishna, Zoroaster, the Buddha, Jesus and Muhammad are seen as teachers from the same divine school, each bringing guidance suited to their age. Their spiritual truths are eternal, while their social laws suited particular times and places.\n\nThe Báb and Bahá'u'lláh are the most recent messengers, though not the last; Bahá'ís believe revelation will continue in the far future. This leads them to treat other religions with deep respect, seeing divisions as the result of human interpretation rather than of the founders' teachings."],
    ["Unity and elected councils", "Bahá'ís teach the oneness of humanity and govern themselves through elected councils with no clergy or campaigning.",
      "If there is one God and one unfolding religion, Bahá'ís reason, there is one human family. From this come principles that sounded radical in the nineteenth century: equality of women and men, universal education, and ending prejudice of race, class, nation or religion. True science and true religion cannot conflict, since both seek truth. Bahá'u'lláh also called for world peace, global governance and an international auxiliary language.\n\nThere are no priests or ministers. Since 1963 the faith has been governed by the Universal House of Justice, a nine-member council in Haifa. Communities elect nine-member spiritual assemblies with no nominations, candidates or campaigning: each voter prays and writes the names of those best suited to serve. Life centres on the Nineteen-Day Feast, as the calendar has nineteen months of nineteen days."],
    ["Temples and persecution", "Bahá'í Houses of Worship are open to everyone, yet Bahá'ís still face persecution in Iran, where the faith began.",
      "Bahá'í Houses of Worship are nine-sided, crowned with a dome, and open to everyone. Services are simple readings and prayers from many faiths' scriptures, often sung by a choir without instruments, with no sermons. The lotus-shaped temple in New Delhi, completed in 1986, is one of India's most visited buildings. Sydney's, in bushland at Ingleside, was dedicated in 1961. The faith's holy places in Haifa and Akka were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2008.\n\nThe faith has spread to almost every country, with followers estimated in the millions. In Iran, though, Bahá'ís are the largest non-Muslim minority and their religion is not recognised. Since 1979, hundreds have been executed and many imprisoned, barred from university or stripped of property, according to the United Nations and human rights groups. Bahá'ís have responded without violence."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Báb teach about his own role?", ["That he was the final messenger of God","That he would found a state in Persia","That he was a forerunner of a greater one","That all earlier religions were false"], 2, "He announced a new era and said he was preparing the way for a far greater messenger."],
    [1, "Where do Bahá'ís believe Bahá'u'lláh first received his revelation?", ["In the underground Black Pit prison in Tehran","In the garden of Ridván in Baghdad","On Mount Carmel, overlooking the bay of Haifa","At his final home near the city of Akka"], 0, "He was imprisoned in 1852, and Bahá'ís believe the revelation came to him there."],
    [2, "What does progressive revelation say about other religions?", ["Earlier teachers were largely mistaken","They share one source, suited to each age","Only the newest religion has any truth","Their spiritual truths are now out of date"], 1, "Messengers are seen as teachers in one school, so Bahá'ís respect other religions."],
    [3, "How are Bahá'í assemblies elected?", ["By open campaigns and speeches","By appointment from a senior priest","By drawing lots among local adults","By secret ballot, with no candidates"], 3, "There are no nominations, candidates or campaigning; each voter prays and writes names."],
    [4, "How are Bahá'ís treated in Iran, the faith's birthplace?", ["They are recognised as the main religion","They face persecution and are not recognised","They hold most senior government posts","They are protected as an official minority"], 1, "The government does not recognise the faith, and many Bahá'ís have been executed or imprisoned."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A faith shaped by Persian prisons now has a temple among Sydney gum trees."});

WB.L("enuma-elish", "beliefs", "intermediate", "Enuma Elish: Babylon's war in heaven",
  "Long before Genesis was written down, Babylonians told how the world was made from the split body of a sea goddess. Their creation epic is also a brilliant piece of political propaganda.",
  [
    ["When on high", "Enuma Elish is the Babylonian creation epic, and it tells how one god became king of all the others.",
      "Ancient Mesopotamian texts were known by their first words, and this one begins \"Enuma elish\", meaning \"When on high\". It opens in a world before the sky or earth had names, when nothing existed but water. The poem fills seven clay tablets written in Akkadian cuneiform.\n\nMost surviving copies date from the first millennium BCE, but the poem is older. Many scholars think it was composed in the late second millennium BCE, perhaps in the reign of Nebuchadnezzar I (twelfth century BCE), though others argue for earlier dates. It is not a neutral account of creation.\n\nIn the mid-nineteenth century, excavations at Nineveh uncovered Ashurbanipal's library, and tablets reached the British Museum. In 1876 George Smith published a translation as The Chaldean Account of Genesis."],
    ["Apsu, Tiamat and Marduk", "The first gods were born from fresh water and salt water, and their noisy descendants led to Marduk's birth.",
      "In the beginning were two primal beings: Apsu, the fresh water beneath the earth, and Tiamat, the salty sea, often imagined as female and sometimes linked with a sea dragon. Their waters mingled, and generations of gods were born.\n\nThe younger gods were noisy and restless, and their racket disturbed Apsu's rest. He resolved to destroy them, despite Tiamat's protests. The wise god Ea discovered the plot, cast a spell that put Apsu to sleep, killed him and built his home upon Apsu's body. There, in that freshwater dwelling, Ea and his wife Damkina had a son: Marduk, a dazzling god with four eyes and four ears, mightier than all his elders."],
    ["Tiamat's army", "Tiamat created monsters for vengeance, and Marduk agreed to fight her only if he became supreme.",
      "Tiamat at first refused to harm her children, but after Apsu's death other gods urged her to strike back. She gave birth to an army of monsters, including serpents with venom for blood, dragons, scorpion-men and raging demons. She chose Kingu as her new husband and commander, and fastened to his chest the Tablet of Destinies, which gave its holder supreme authority over the universe.\n\nThe other gods were terrified. Ea and then the sky god Anu went out to face her and turned back. Finally the assembly turned to young Marduk. He agreed to fight on one condition: if he won, his word would decide destinies, and he would be supreme among the gods."],
    ["A world from a body", "Marduk slew Tiamat and built the ordered cosmos from the two halves of her body.",
      "Armed with a bow, a mace, a net and the winds, Marduk rode out in a storm chariot. When Tiamat opened her mouth to swallow him, he drove in an evil wind that swelled her body, then shot an arrow that split her heart. He caught her helpers in his net and took the Tablet of Destinies from Kingu.\n\nThen he began to build. He split Tiamat's body in two like a fish for drying, raising one half to form the sky and laying out the other as the earth. From her eyes flowed the Tigris and Euphrates. He set the stars and constellations, fixed the calendar, and gave the moon its phases. The cosmos was ordered from the body of chaos."],
    ["Humans, work and power", "Humans were made from a rebel god's blood to serve the gods, and the epic promoted Babylon's own god, Marduk.",
      "With the world built, someone had to do the endless labour of maintaining it. The gods blamed Kingu for the rebellion and put him to death. From his blood Ea fashioned humankind, given the gods' work so the gods could rest. People exist to serve the gods by tending temples and offering food. The gods built Babylon and its temple, the Esagila, for Marduk.\n\nThe epic raised Marduk, Babylon's patron, above older gods, mirroring the city's rise, and was recited at the Akitu new year festival. When the Assyrians adopted it, some versions replaced Marduk with their god Ashur. Genesis likewise opens with primeval waters and a separation of sky from earth. Some scholars link the Hebrew tehom, \"the deep\", to Tiamat, though how much the biblical writers knew is debated."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is Enuma Elish not a neutral account of creation?", ["It was written by a foreign historian","It covers only the creation of animals","It makes one god king of all the others","It was copied from the Book of Genesis"], 2, "The poem explains creation while elevating Marduk to supreme rule."],
    [1, "What triggered Apsu's decision to destroy the younger gods?", ["They had stolen his Tablet of Destinies","They were so noisy he could not rest","They had refused to bow down to him","They plotted to overthrow Tiamat"], 1, "Their racket disturbed Apsu, and the plot that followed led to his death and Marduk's birth."],
    [2, "What condition did Marduk set before fighting Tiamat?", ["That he would rule and decide destinies","That Ea would fight beside him","That the gods build him a temple first","That Kingu be spared"], 0, "Marduk's price for risking battle was supremacy among the gods."],
    [3, "How did Marduk make the sky and earth?", ["By drawing them from a primal egg","By splitting Tiamat's body in two","By pouring out the waters of Apsu","By casting the Tablet of Destinies down"], 1, "He halved her body like a fish for drying, making one half the sky and the other the earth."],
    [4, "What does the Assyrians' swap of Marduk for Ashur suggest?", ["Marduk had been forgotten across Mesopotamia","The epic was a claim about whose god rules","Ashur had always been the god of Babylon","The Assyrians could not read Akkadian"], 1, "Swapping the national god suggests the epic was read as a claim about who rules."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Babylon's creation story makes the world from a goddess's body, and a political point."});

WB.L("aztec-gods", "beliefs", "intermediate", "The Aztec gods and the Fifth Sun",
  "The Mexica believed they lived under the fifth sun the gods had made, and that it moved only because gods had given their lives. Their religion was built around keeping it in the sky.",
  [
    ["The Mexica and their city", "The people we call the Aztecs called themselves the Mexica and founded Tenochtitlan on a lake island.",
      "The name \"Aztec\" was popularised much later. The builders of Tenochtitlan called themselves the Mexica, and their name lives on in Mexico. In their own tradition, they migrated south from a homeland called Aztlan, guided by their patron god Huitzilopochtli. He promised they would know their new home when they saw an eagle perched on a cactus growing from a rock.\n\nThey found that sign on a marshy island in Lake Texcoco, where they founded Tenochtitlan, traditionally in 1325. The eagle on the cactus is now at the centre of Mexico's flag. Within two centuries the island had become one of the largest cities in the world, linked to the shore by causeways."],
    ["Four suns and the Fifth", "The Mexica believed the world had been destroyed four times, and the present Fifth Sun began when gods sacrificed themselves.",
      "In Mexica cosmology, time moved in great ages called Suns, each a separate creation that ended in catastrophe. In one widely told version, the first world was destroyed by jaguars, the second by winds, the third by a rain of fire and the fourth by a flood. Accounts differ, since our sources were written after the Spanish conquest. The Fifth Sun, Nahui Ollin, was also destined to end, many believed, in earthquakes.\n\nAfter the fourth world, the gods gathered in darkness at Teotihuacan. Proud Tecciztecatl lost his nerve four times; humble, sickly Nanahuatzin leapt into the fire and rose as the sun. Ashamed, Tecciztecatl followed. A god threw a rabbit into his face, dimming him into the moon. Even then the sun would not move until the other gods gave their lives."],
    ["War, rain and rivals", "The Templo Mayor honoured both war and rain, while Quetzalcoatl and Tezcatlipoca were rival creator gods.",
      "At the heart of Tenochtitlan stood the Templo Mayor, a stepped pyramid topped by two shrines. One honoured Huitzilopochtli, the Mexica's patron god of the sun and war. The other honoured Tlaloc, an ancient god of rain and fertility. Together they represented war and agriculture, the twin foundations of the empire. Huitzilopochtli was born fully armed, defeated his siblings who plotted against his mother Coatlicue, and cast his dismembered sister Coyolxauhqui down the hill.\n\nQuetzalcoatl, the Feathered Serpent, was linked with wind, learning and the morning star. His rival Tezcatlipoca, the \"Smoking Mirror\", was a powerful god of night, fate and sorcery. In one cycle of tales they alternately create and destroy the earlier Suns."],
    ["Debt and sacrifice", "Because gods died to set the sun moving, the Mexica believed humans owed a debt repaid in offerings and blood.",
      "Mexica sacrifice followed from their myths. The gods gave their lives to create and power the Fifth Sun, so humans owed a debt that had to be repaid. Worshippers offered incense, food, flowers and their own blood, drawn with cactus spines or bone points.\n\nMost dramatically, priests sacrificed human beings, many of them war captives, at ceremonies across the ritual year. Spanish accounts of the scale were sometimes exaggerated, and historians long debated them, but archaeology has confirmed that sacrifice was real and significant. Since 2015, excavations in Mexico City have uncovered parts of the Huei Tzompantli, a great rack and tower built with the skulls of hundreds of individuals, near the Templo Mayor."],
    ["Myths after the conquest", "The story that the Mexica thought Cortés was Quetzalcoatl is now widely doubted, but much of their culture survived.",
      "Hernán Cortés conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521, with tens of thousands of Indigenous allies. A famous story claims the emperor Moctezuma believed Cortés was the returning Quetzalcoatl. Many historians now doubt this, since it appears mainly in sources written decades after the conquest, shaped by Spanish and Indigenous writers trying to explain what had happened. One tale has Quetzalcoatl departing east, promising in some versions to return.\n\nThe Mexica religion was suppressed, its temples demolished and a cathedral built near the ruins. Yet much survived. In 1978 electricity workers in Mexico City uncovered a huge carved stone of the dismembered Coyolxauhqui, leading to the full excavation of the Templo Mayor. More than a million and a half people in Mexico still speak Nahuatl, the Mexica language."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What sign told the Mexica where to found their city?", ["A river flowing into a great lake","An eagle perched on a cactus on a rock","A jaguar crouched on a stone pyramid","A rabbit marked on the moon's face"], 1, "Huitzilopochtli's promised sign appeared on an island in Lake Texcoco, and it is now on Mexico's flag."],
    [1, "What did the Mexica believe about the Fifth Sun?", ["It was certain to last for all time","It was made by humans to honour the gods","It began in divine sacrifice and could fail","It had already ended in a great flood"], 2, "The world was seen as fragile, and the sun only moved because gods gave their lives."],
    [2, "What did the twin shrines of the Templo Mayor represent?", ["Two rival kings of the empire","War and farming","Day and night as opposing forces","The Spanish and the Mexica peoples"], 1, "Huitzilopochtli stood for sun and war, and Tlaloc for rain and fertility."],
    [3, "Why did the Mexica believe in sacrifice?", ["Humans owed a debt to gods who died","Spanish priests required it","It was only used for entertainment","Gods demanded it as punishment for old crimes"], 0, "Offerings and blood repaid the gods who gave their lives to power the Fifth Sun."],
    [4, "Why do many historians doubt the claim that Moctezuma thought Cortés was Quetzalcoatl?", ["Quetzalcoatl never existed in Mexica myth","It appears mainly in much later sources","Cortés rejected it in his own letters","Moctezuma never actually met Cortés"], 1, "The tale comes from later accounts shaped by writers explaining the conquest."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why the Mexica thought the sun itself needed their help to rise."});

WB.L("yoruba-orisha", "beliefs", "intermediate", "The orishas: Yoruba gods of two continents",
  "From the cities of south-western Nigeria to the beaches of Brazil and the streets of Havana, millions of people honour the orishas, divine forces of thunder, rivers, iron and the sea.",
  [
    ["The Yoruba world", "Yoruba religion centres on a distant creator and the orishas, divine forces who govern nature and life.",
      "The Yoruba are one of Africa's largest ethnic groups, numbering tens of millions, mostly in south-western Nigeria and neighbouring Benin and Togo. Their traditional religion, Ìṣẹ̀ṣe, recognises a supreme creator, Olodumare, who is vast and distant and not usually approached directly.\n\nInstead, people relate to the orishas, divine beings who act as intermediaries and govern particular forces of nature and areas of life. Some are seen as forces present since creation, others as deified ancestors and kings. There is also àṣẹ, the vital power that makes things happen, flowing through gods, people, words and objects. Today many Yoruba are Christian or Muslim, but traditional practice and its influence remain strong."],
    ["Land from water", "In Yoruba tradition the orisha Obatala helped make dry land at Ile-Ife, the sacred city.",
      "One widely told creation story begins with the world as water and marsh. Olodumare sends the orisha Obatala down from the sky on a golden chain, carrying a snail shell full of earth and a bird, often described as a hen or cockerel. Obatala pours out the earth, and the bird scratches and scatters it, spreading dry land.\n\nIn some versions Obatala becomes drunk on palm wine and falls asleep, and his rival Oduduwa completes the task. Obatala is also said to shape human bodies from clay, and some traditions explain disabilities as the result of his drunkenness. His followers therefore avoid palm wine and protect people with disabilities. The place where land first appeared is Ile-Ife, still regarded as sacred."],
    ["A pantheon of personalities", "Each orisha has its own domain, symbols and devotees, from Ogun's iron to Oshun's sweet waters.",
      "The orishas are vivid personalities. Obatala, associated with white cloth and coolness, stands for purity, wisdom and calm judgement. Ogun, orisha of iron, tools and war, is honoured by blacksmiths, hunters and, today, drivers and mechanics. Shango, lord of thunder and lightning, is remembered as a fiery king of Oyo, and his symbol is a double-headed axe.\n\nOshun, orisha of sweet waters, love, beauty and fertility, is linked with the Osun River, and her sacred grove at Osogbo is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Yemoja is a great mother associated with water. Eshu, or Elegba, the trickster messenger, guards crossroads, carries offerings, and must be honoured first in many ceremonies."],
    ["Ifá: reading destiny", "Ifá divination uses 256 patterns, each tied to memorised verses, to guide decisions.",
      "Facing a serious decision, illness or trouble, a person may consult a babalawo, a priest of Ifá. Ifá is linked with Orunmila, the orisha of wisdom and destiny. The babalawo casts a divining chain, or manipulates sacred palm nuts, to produce one of 256 possible patterns called odu.\n\nEach odu is associated with a large collection of memorised verses, stories and proverbs. The priest recites them, and priest and client together interpret which passage speaks to the situation, often followed by recommended offerings. Training takes many years. UNESCO has recognised Ifá divination as part of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, and its verses are treasured as a vast store of Yoruba history, values and wisdom."],
    ["Across the Atlantic", "Enslaved Yoruba people carried the orishas to Cuba and Brazil, where Santería and Candomblé grew.",
      "During the Atlantic slave trade, especially in its final decades, large numbers of Yoruba people were taken to the Americas, particularly Cuba and Brazil. Forbidden from openly practising their religion and pressured to become Catholic, many found ways to keep the orishas alive. Each orisha came to be linked with a Catholic saint of similar imagery: Shango with St Barbara, whose symbols include lightning, and Oshun with Cuba's Virgin of Charity. Scholars debate how far this was disguise and how far blending.\n\nFrom this grew Santería (Lucumí) in Cuba and Candomblé in Brazil, with drumming, dance, possession and initiation. Yemoja became Yemayá in Cuba and Iemanjá in Brazil, queen of the sea. On 2 February crowds in Salvador carry gifts to the sea for her, and many Brazilians toss flowers into the waves on New Year's Eve."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do Yoruba people usually relate to the creator Olodumare?", ["Through the orishas as intermediaries","By offering sacrifices every morning","By consulting only the Ifá priests","Directly, in daily personal prayer"], 0, "Olodumare is distant and not usually approached directly, so people turn to the orishas."],
    [1, "What did Obatala's bird do in the creation story?", ["Carried the first sun into the sky","Scattered the earth to spread dry land","Led him on the path to Ile-Ife","Released the golden chain from above"], 1, "Obatala poured out earth from a snail shell, and the bird spread it into dry land."],
    [2, "Which orisha is honoured by blacksmiths and mechanics?", ["Oshun","Shango","Ogun","Yemoja"], 2, "Ogun is the orisha of iron, tools and war, so metalworkers and now drivers honour him."],
    [3, "How does an Ifá priest give guidance?", ["By predicting the future from the stars","By reading lines on the client's palm","By applying a written code of law","By reciting verses tied to a cast pattern"], 3, "Each of the 256 odu has memorised verses, and priest and client interpret which fits the situation."],
    [4, "Why were orishas linked with Catholic saints in the Americas?", ["Church officials assigned saints to orishas at random","Orishas shared imagery or qualities with certain saints","Catholic missionaries had already adopted the orishas","Yoruba priests first learned of the saints in Africa"], 1, "Similar symbols, like Shango's lightning and St Barbara's, let the orishas live on under pressure."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"How a West African religion survived slavery and now fills Brazil's beaches each New Year."});

WB.L("making-the-bible", "beliefs", "advanced", "How the Bible became a book",
  "The Bible was not written at once, nor agreed on in a single meeting. It grew over a thousand years, and Christians still do not all have the same list of books.",
  [
    ["A library, not a book", "The Bible is a library of writings from many authors, and different communities keep different official lists, called canons.",
      "The word Bible comes from the Greek ta biblia, \"the books\". It is a library, not a single work, holding law, history, poetry, prophecy, proverbs, letters and visions. Most of it was written in Hebrew and Greek, with some Aramaic, over a span usually put at more than a thousand years.\n\nA canon is the official list of books a community accepts as scripture, from a Greek word for a measuring rod. The Jewish Bible has 24 books. Protestant Bibles have 66, Catholic Bibles 73, and Eastern Orthodox and Oriental churches more again. Even chapters and verses are late additions: chapters date from the thirteenth century, New Testament verse numbers from 1551."],
    ["Torah, Prophets, Writings", "The Hebrew Bible took shape in three parts, with the Torah recognised first and the Writings settled last.",
      "Jewish scripture is called the Tanakh, an acronym of its three parts: Torah (the Teaching, the five books of Moses), Nevi'im (the Prophets) and Ketuvim (the Writings, including Psalms, Proverbs and Job). Scholars generally think the Torah was recognised as authoritative first, probably by around the fifth or fourth century BCE, then the Prophets.\n\nThe Writings stayed open longer. Rabbinic texts record debates over whether Ecclesiastes and the Song of Songs belonged, and the list seems to have been broadly settled in the early centuries CE. Protestant Old Testaments hold the same material, counted as 39 books rather than 24 because Samuel, Kings and the twelve minor prophets are divided differently."],
    ["Why Christian lists differ", "Christian Bibles differ because of extra books in the Greek Septuagint, which Protestants, Catholics and Orthodox treat differently.",
      "From the third century BCE, Greek-speaking Jews in Alexandria translated their scriptures into Greek. A legend says seventy-two scholars finished in seventy-two days, hence the name Septuagint, from the Latin for seventy. The Greek collections also held books outside the Jewish canon, such as Tobit, Judith, Sirach and Maccabees. Early Christians mostly read Greek and used it widely.\n\nCatholics call these extra books deuterocanonical, and Protestants call them the Apocrypha. In 1534 Martin Luther placed them in a separate section, useful but not equal to scripture. In 1546 the Council of Trent affirmed them as canonical. Orthodox churches accept more again, and the Ethiopian Orthodox canon is largest, usually counted at around 81 books."],
    ["The Dead Sea Scrolls", "Scrolls found near Qumran from 1947 pushed the manuscript record of the Hebrew Bible back about a thousand years.",
      "In 1947 Bedouin shepherds exploring caves near Qumran, on the north-western shore of the Dead Sea, found jars holding ancient scrolls. Over the next decade around 900 manuscripts, mostly fragments, were recovered from eleven caves. Dating mainly from the third century BCE to the first century CE, they include copies or fragments of every Hebrew Bible book except Esther.\n\nUntil then, the oldest complete Hebrew Bible manuscripts were medieval, such as the Leningrad Codex of about 1008 CE. The Great Isaiah Scroll, roughly a thousand years older, proved remarkably close to the medieval text. Other scrolls show variant versions, revealing that some books still circulated in more than one form."],
    ["Not decided at Nicaea", "No single council chose the New Testament; the list formed gradually, and the claim that Nicaea decided it is a myth.",
      "Paul's letters, usually dated to the 50s CE, are the earliest New Testament writings. Most scholars date the four gospels to roughly 70 to 100 CE. By around 200 CE the gospels and Paul's letters were widely accepted, while Hebrews, James, 2 Peter, Jude and Revelation stayed disputed longer. The Shepherd of Hermas was read in some churches but left out.\n\nA popular claim, spread by novels such as The Da Vinci Code, says Constantine and the Council of Nicaea in 325 CE picked the books. There is no evidence for it. Nicaea dealt with the nature of Christ, the date of Easter and church discipline. In 367 CE Athanasius wrote the earliest known list matching today's 27 books, and councils at Hippo (393) and Carthage (397) endorsed similar lists."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a canon of scripture?", ["The oldest surviving manuscript of a text","An official list of books a community accepts","A translation of scripture into another language","A commentary explaining difficult passages"], 1, "A canon is a community's official list of accepted books, and different communities settled on different canons."],
    [1, "Which part of the Hebrew Bible was recognised as authoritative first?", ["The Writings","The Prophets","The Apocrypha","The Torah"], 3, "The Torah was recognised first, then the Prophets, while the Writings stayed open the longest."],
    [2, "Why do Catholic and Protestant Bibles differ in length?", ["Catholics include extra books from the Greek Septuagint","Protestants added later gospels to their New Testament","Catholics left out several of Paul's letters","Protestants added books found at Qumran"], 0, "Extra books in the Greek Septuagint became a lasting point of difference between Christian traditions."],
    [3, "What did the Great Isaiah Scroll show?", ["Isaiah was rewritten in the Middle Ages","Isaiah was originally written in Greek","Its wording closely matched medieval copies","Isaiah was not part of the Hebrew Bible"], 2, "The scroll showed that the Hebrew text had been copied faithfully over a thousand years, though other scrolls show variant forms."],
    [4, "How was the New Testament list actually settled?", ["By one vote at the Council of Nicaea","Gradually, as churches came to agree","By an order from Emperor Constantine","By a decree from the bishop of Rome"], 1, "The canon formed slowly across regions; Nicaea dealt with other matters entirely."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"No emperor and no single council chose the books of the Bible. So who did?"});

